.TH COIL 1
.SH NAME
coil \- modal text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B coil
[
.B -D
] [
.B -s
] [
.B -f
.I font
]
.I file\fR[\fP\fB:\fP\fIN\fR]\fP
.PP
The file argument is required; without it
.I coil
prints a usage message and exits.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Coil
is a modal text editor in its own
.IR draw (3)
window, in the style of Helix and Kakoune: select first, then act on the selection.
It runs unchanged in
.IR rio (1),
in rini and nested.
There is always at least one selection of at least one rune, and
the cursor is a selection of one rune.
Motions move or extend the selections; verbs
.RB ( d ,
.BR c ,
.BR y ,
.BR p )
act on them.
The text is held as bytes: invalid UTF-8 and NUL bytes survive a load and a save unchanged.
.PP
The file is opened at once; a name that does not exist starts an empty buffer
that is created on the first write.
.I File\fB:\fIN
opens the file at line
.IR N .
The last row of the window is the status line.
It shows the mode, the file name, a modified mark, the line and column of the cursor,
the number of selections and the last message.
.PP
The options are
.TF "-f font"
.TP
.B -D
Debug: trace resizes, keys and mouse events to standard error.
.TP
.B -s
Scroll the window with a single draw request when the text moves by a few lines,
instead of repainting every row.
.TP
.BI -f " font
Use
.I font
(fixed width) instead of the default
.BR /lib/font/bit/vga/unicode.font .
.PD
.SS Normal mode
A number before a key repeats it.
.TF "mi\fIc\fP, ma\fIc\fP"
.TP
.B h j k l
Move by one rune or line; the arrow keys do the same.
.TP
.B w b e
Move to the next word start (selecting the word and the blanks after it), the previous word start, the word end.
.TP
.B W B E
The same with only blanks as word separators.
.TP
.B H L
Go to the line start or end; Home and End do the same.
.TP
.B gg ge
Go to the start or the end of the file.
.TP
.B G
Go to line
.I count
(the last line without a count).
.TP
.B ^D ^U
Half a page down or up.
.TP
.B ^F ^B
A page down or up; PgDn and PgUp do the same.
.TP
.B x
Select the whole line, including its newline; repeated, extend by a line.
.TP
.B %
Select the whole file.
.TP
.B ;
Collapse each selection to its cursor rune.
.TP
.B ,
Flip each selection: swap anchor and head.
.TP
.BR mi c ", " ma c
Select the text inside or around the object
.IR c :
.B w W p ( ) b { } B [ ] " ' `
(word, big word, paragraph, brackets, quotes).
.TP
.B v
Enter Select mode.
.TP
.B i a
Enter Insert mode before or after the selections.
.TP
.B I A
Enter Insert mode at the start or end of the line.
.TP
.B o O
Open a new line below or above and enter Insert mode.
.TP
.B d
Delete the selected text into the snarf buffer.
.TP
.B c
Delete the selected text into the snarf buffer and enter Insert mode.
.TP
.B y
Yank the selections into the snarf buffer.
.TP
.B p P
Paste after or before the selections.
.TP
.B R
Replace the selections with the snarf buffer.
.TP
.BI r c
Replace every rune of the selections by
.IR c ;
Esc cancels.
.TP
.B u U
Undo and redo.
One command, including a multi-selection edit or a structural command, is one step;
a whole Insert mode session is one step.
.TP
.B :
Open the command line (see
.BR Commands ).
.TP
.B / ? n N *
Search forward, search backward, repeat in the same or the opposite direction,
search for the selected text literally.
.TP
.B Esc
Drop all selections except the primary.
.TP
.B ^S
Save the file.
.TP
.B ^Q
Quit; when the buffer is modified, a second press quits and discards the changes.
.PD
.SS Select mode
Entered with
.BR v .
The motions above extend the selections instead of moving them: the anchor stays
and the head follows.
A search keeps the anchor.
Every other key of Normal mode works the same,
and Esc leaves Select mode and keeps the selections.
.SS Insert mode
Printable keys, Enter and Tab are typed at every caret.
.TF "Backspace"
.TP
.B Esc
Return to Normal mode.
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the rune before each caret.
.TP
.B ^W
Delete the word before each caret.
.TP
.B ^U
Delete back to the start of the line.
.TP
.B arrows
Move the carets; Home, End, PgUp and PgDn too.
.TP
.B ^S
Save the file.
.PD
.PP
Tab inserts a real tab in every language except Markdown, which gets spaces.
The tab width is 4.
.SS Command line
.B :
in Normal or Select mode, and
.B /
and
.B ?
for search, open a line in the status row.
Enter runs it and Esc cancels.
Backspace erases a rune (on an empty line it closes the prompt),
^W a word, ^U the whole line.
Up and Down recall earlier lines; there is a separate history for
.B :
and for searches.
.SS Mouse
Button 1 clicks to place the cursor and drags to select;
a double click selects a word.
The wheel scrolls by three lines.
A click in the line-number gutter places the cursor at the start of that line.
.SS Commands
Paths are literal; there is no shell and no expansion.
.TF ":w\ file"
.TP
.B :w
Write the file.
It refuses when the file changed on disk since coil read it
.RB ( :w!
overwrites).
.TP
.B :w!
Write even if the file changed on disk.
.TP
.BI :w " file
Write a copy.
.TP
.B :q
Quit; refused when modified.
.TP
.B :q!
Quit and discard changes.
.TP
.B :wq
Write and quit.
.TP
.BI :e " file
Open another file;
.IB file : N
jumps to line
.IR N .
.TP
.B :e!
Reload the file, or with a file name open it discarding changes.
.TP
.B :N
Go to line
.IR N .
.TP
.B ":num\fR [\fPon\fR|\fPoff\fR]\fP"
Show or hide the line-number gutter (no argument toggles it).
It is on by default and is not saved; it is hidden in windows too narrow for it.
.TP
.B ":lang\fR [\fPc\fR|\fPrc\fR|\fPmkfile\fR|\fPmarkdown\fR|\fPplain\fR]\fP"
Force the language of the buffer (highlighting and tab setting), or with no argument report it.
.PD
.PP
The language is detected from the file name when the file is opened:
.B .c .h .y .l
are C;
.B .rc
and files starting with
.B #!/bin/rc
or
.B #!/rc/bin/rc
are rc;
.BR mkfile ,
.BR mkfile.* ,
.BR *.mk ,
.BR mkone ,
.BR mkmany ,
.B mklib
and
.B mksyslib
are mkfiles;
.B .md
is Markdown; anything else is plain.
Writing an unnamed buffer with
.B :w
.I name
detects the language again from the new name and overrides a forced language.
.SS Structural commands
Any other line is a command in the style of
.IR sam (1),
which selects first and then acts:
.IP
.B x/\fIre\fB/
.br
.B y/\fIre\fB/
.br
.B g/\fIre\fB/
.br
.B v/\fIre\fB/
.PP
.B x
makes each match of the expression in each current selection the new selection;
.B y
the text between the matches;
.B g
keeps a selection that contains a match and
.B v
one that does not.
Without an expression
.B x
and
.B y
iterate over lines.
They chain, and end in one of
.TF "s/re/text/g"
.TP
.B d
delete the selections
.TP
.BI c/ text /
replace them with
.I text
.TP
.BI i/ text /
insert before them
.TP
.BI a/ text /
insert after them
.TP
.BI s/ re / text /\fR[\fPg\fR]\fP
substitute the first (or with
.BR g ,
every) match; in
.I text
.B &
and
.B \e0
are the match and
.B \e1
to
.B \e9
the groups.
.PD
.PP
The terminals
.BR d ,
.BR c ,
.BR i ,
.B a
and
.B s
also work alone, on each current selection,
or on the whole file after a
.B %
or
.B ,
prefix; for example
.B :%s/foo/bar/g
replaces every
.B foo
in the file with
.BR bar .
.PP
The starting selections are the current ones, or the whole file after a
.B %
or
.B ,
prefix.
For example
.B :%x/foo/ c/bar/
replaces every
.B foo
and
.B :%x g/TODO/ d
deletes the lines holding
.BR TODO .
An empty expression
.RB ( x// )
reuses the last pattern.
In text,
.B \en
is a newline,
.B \et
a tab and
.B \e/
the delimiter.
All edits of a command are made against the original text and applied together:
the whole command is one undo step, and the new selections cover the changed text.
A command may make at most 250000 changes;
beyond 50000 the status row first shows
.BR "applying N changes..." .
Esc or Del interrupts a running search or command, leaving the text unchanged.
Searches and commands run in slices between input events, so the window stays alive.
.SS Regular expressions
Patterns are the
.IR regexp (6)
dialect of the system library: literals,
.B .
.B [...]
.B [^...]
.B *
.B +
.B ?
.B |
.B ( )
.B ^
.B $ .
.B \en
and
.B \et
are translated by coil to a newline and a tab.
There is no
.B \ed
.B \ew
or
.BR \es ,
no
.BR {n,m} ,
no non-greedy
.B *?
and no
.BR (?:...) ;
they do not fail, they match the wrong thing.
A dot and a negated class never match a newline;
.B ^
and
.B $
match at every line start and end.
.SS Highlighting
C, rc, mkfile and Markdown are highlighted.
C has keywords, the types of the Plan 9 headers (including
.BR u8int ,
.BR u16int ,
.BR u32int ,
.BR u64int ,
.B s8int
to
.BR s64int ,
.B uchar
and
.BR schar ),
strings, characters, numbers, comments and preprocessor lines.
rc has keywords, variables, quotes, comments and redirections; a mkfile has
targets, recipes, assignments, variables and conditionals;
Markdown has headings, emphasis, strong text, code spans and fences, links and list marks.
The cursor shows the partner of a bracket
.B ( ) [ ] { }
under it in the
.B match
style when it lies within 500 lines.
Trailing blanks are drawn in the
.B trailing
style (except on the line being typed on).
.SS Styles
Colours come from the theme file
.BR $home/lib/coil/theme ,
one
.I name
.B fg=#rrggbb bg=#rrggbb
and optionally
.B underline
per line, as in
.IR cell (2).
Without it a built-in dark theme is used.
Every style is listed here:
.TF selection
.TP
.B default
text and background
.TP
.B selection
the overlay mixed over the selected text
.TP
.B cursor
the cursor cell
.TP
.B status
the status row
.TP
.B message
messages in the status row
.TP
.B error
errors
.TP
.B ctrl
control characters
.TP
.B invalid
bytes that are not UTF-8
.TP
.B keyword type comment string number preproc
the C tokens and, where they fit, the other languages
.TP
.B variable target recipe
rc and mkfile variables, mkfile targets, mkfile recipe lines
.TP
.B heading emph strong code link listmark
Markdown (headings and strong text are underlined)
.TP
.B match
the bracket partner
.TP
.B trailing
trailing blanks
.TP
.B linenr
the line-number gutter
.PD
.PP
A light, acme-like theme is in
.B lib/theme.light
in the source tree; to use it:
.IP
.B cp lib/theme.light $home/lib/coil/theme
.PP
The theme is read at startup.
A bad line shows as
.B theme:LINE: message
and the built-in colours stay.
.SH FILES
.TF "$home/lib/coil/theme"
.TP
.B $home/lib/coil/theme
optional theme
.TP
.B /dev/snarf
the clipboard, opened afresh for each yank and paste
.SH SOURCE
.B src
in the coil repository.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR sam (1),
.IR acme (1),
.IR rio (1),
.IR cell (2),
.IR regexp (6)
.SH BUGS
A hangup (closing the window or the window system exiting) loses unsaved edits; there are no recovery files.
.PP
Combining marks are not drawn (no combining marks).
Fullwidth glyphs (fullwidth forms) are drawn as boxes with the default font.
Long lines are not wrapped; at most 65536 bytes of a line are shown (Maxline), and a line
is highlighted only up to its first 4096 bytes.
.PP
After a jump far into a file, a comment or fence opened more than 2000 lines earlier is
not seen until an edit above it.
A block comment that ends inside a preprocessor line is not recognised.
.PP
Not recognised by the highlighter: rc here-documents, mkfile backslash continuation lines,
and in Markdown setext headings, indented code, reference links, HTML and nested emphasis.
.PP
The bracket match looks at most 500 lines (and 64 KiB) from the cursor.
A single line longer than the 256 KiB search window cannot be matched across the window edge.
The snarf buffer of
.IR rio (1)
holds 100 KiB; larger yanks stay in coil's own register.
